1 NAME Uvaldo Isador /Sanchez/
2 GIVN Uvaldo Isador
2 _MARNM Sanchez
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1890
2 PLAC Valencia
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 New Mexico
3 STAE NM
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1950
2 ADDR
3 CITY Los Angeles
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Rancher
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Information from Joseph H.Sanchez, great grandson
2 CONT
2 CONT After suffering during the Great Depression, the family abandoned the family ranch in Valencia, New Mexico and sold what they could and bought a new Packard car and moved to Los Angeles. Uvaldo relocated to Los Angeles with all hi
2 CONC s sons and daughters and also his three sisters and brother, whose names were Alicia, Lillian, Flora and Fred. His brother and sisters lived in a spanish  stucco house on Normandy Blvd and they all lived that house until they died
2 CONC  . Uvaldo did not live with them, he lived alone in boarding houses in the area around the old city plaza in downtown Los Angeles and found infrequent work interpreting for the spanish speakers at the county courthouse. None of th
2 CONC em ever returned to New Mexico. Uvaldo had four sons and two daughters all of them lived and raised families in Los Angeles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family story says that Ubaldo soon remarried after the death of his first wife Rosalia.The woman he married was a widow that had a couple of children of her own. Family says that Ubaldo did not have any children with her. The 
2 CONC marriage ended somehow and that is when Ublado left her the hacienda and whatever else and came to Los Angeles with the extended family.
2 CONT
2 CONT I could not find anyone who could tell of the hard times the family struggled with during the Great Depression of the 1930's and how the results of the hardships caused the entire family to abandon the hacienda leaving the second 
2 CONC wife to carry on by herself and her children. A cousin tells a story of how her father Salomon Sanchez, a son of Ubaldo went back to visit the hacienda and chapel in the mid sixties and of how the second wife still lived there and
2 CONC  showed the family the house and they visited the chapel on the hacienda where the funeral of Rosalia was held. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I would like to thank Tina Perez (Sanchez) for the wedding photo of Ubaldo and Rosela.
