2 DATE 9 JUL 1945
2 PLAC Fukuoka No. 17 Branch Prisoner of War Camp, Mitsui Kozan Miike Kogyo-Sho Coal Mine
2 ADDR
3 CITY Shinminato-machi
3 STAE Fukuoka-ken
3 CTRY Japan
1 OCCU US Army Air Corps, 24th Pursuit Group, V Bomb Commmand, AC 34th Bomb Squadron
1 TITL Pvt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.cfmf.net/honoree.aspx?id=866 Colorado Freedom Memorial]
2 CONT * [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=63341777 Find A Grave]
2 CONT * [https://wartimememoriesproject.com/ww2/view.php?uid=214958 Wartime Memorials Project]
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page for Fukuoka 17:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuoka_17
2 CONT
2 CONT Fukuoka #17 Branch Prisoner of War Camp was a Japanese Prisoner-of-war camp located at the Mitsui Kozan Miike Kogyo-Sho coal mine and Mitsui Zinc Foundry in Shinminato-machi, Omuta-shi, Fukuoka-ken, Japan, during World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Camp was opened 7 August 1943 and was gradually populated by about 1,857[1] prisoners of war of mixed nationalities, mostly Australian, American, British and Dutch. The British, Dutch and Australians were survivors of the Burm
2 CONC a Railway construction in Thailand (Siam). The last group to arrive were all Australians, in June 1945; they had formerly been held in the Fukuoka 13-D Oita POW camp.
2 CONT
2 CONT The site was originally the labourers' quarters built by the Mitsui Coal Mining Company owned by the Baron Mitsui. The camp was gradually expanded with further buildings constructed over the years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Description'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The camp measured 200 yards by 1000 yards and was surrounded by a 12 feet high wooden fence fixed with heavy gauge wire. There were 33 barracks, all one story buildings 120' x 16' with ten rooms to a barracks. Constructed of wood 
2 CONC with tight tar paper roofs, and windows with panes. Officers were billeted three or four per room measuring 9' x 10', with four to six enlisted men accommodated in rooms of same size. There was no heating whatsoever, which was a s
2 CONC erious problem in the winter months as the men were living on starvation diets. Each room had one 15-watt light bulb.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT There were two meals served each day, usually one cup of rice and some radish soup. Protein was rarely provided.
2 CONT
2 CONT The camp commandant was Asao Fukuhara, who was executed after the war for war crimes. The camp doctor was an unidentified Japanese surgeon who forced men to work even when critically ill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron Mitsui's company leased the POWs from the Japanese Army, who received payment from the Company of about 20 yen per day. The American, Australian and Dutch POWs all worked in the Mitsui coal mine whilst all the British worked
2 CONC  in the nearby Mitsui zinc foundry. Pay for privates and NCOs was 10 yen per day and all POWs received about 5 cigarettes each day.
2 CONT
2 CONT The camp was liberated on 2 September 1945, by which time most of the POWs were in a desperate state of health. Many POWs were suffering from severe beri-beri and on average had lost about 60 lb (27 kg).[2] Camp survivors were eva
2 CONC cuated via the destroyed Nagasaki about ten days after liberation.
2 CONT
