1 CONT Listed as "Vecinos de Ojo Caliente."
1 CONT Padrino: Manuel Martin.
1 CONT Madrina: Maria Vitoria Martin.
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002279631872@ NOTE Baptized on December 31, 1843 (13 days) at Abiquiu, New Mexico.
1 CONT Listed as "Vecinos de Rio de Chama."
1 CONT Padrino: Luis Antonio Duran.
1 CONT Madrina: Maria Magdalena Mascarenas.
0 @N6000000002065613274@ NOTE 1920 Census, Otero CO, Colorado ED 164 Sheet 4B
0 @N6000000002279602678@ NOTE Baptized on September 13, 1842 (6 days) in Abiquiu, New Mexico.
1 CONT Padrino: Antonio Quintana.
1 CONT Madrina: Maria Rafaela Martin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Felipe Nerio Mestas was shot and killed in El Moro, Colorado by the husband of an anglo woman he had befriended.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Trinidad Daily Advertiser, Tuesday, March 11, 1884:
1 CONT Murder At Pulaski
1 CONT "A Quarrel Between two Old Citizens and Near Neighbors Settled with the Pistol"
1 CONT At 4 o'clock Saturday afternoon Felipe Mestas went to the house of John Jackson and a personal quarrel in the house of the latter resulted.  As a grudge of longstanding occasioned the quarrel feeling was quite bitter.  Jackson ord
1 CONC ered Mestas out of the house but Mestas refused to go.  Then Jackson caught Mestas by the lapel of the coat; Mestas likewise catching Jackson in a similar manner.  In less time than can be told a ball from Jackson's revolver pierc
1 CONC ed Mestas just below the left nipple.  As Mestas turned his face from Jackson the latter kept up his firing, shooting his antagonist once through the body and twice in the back of the neck.  Squire Reck held the inquest yesterday.
1 CONC   Jackson has  not been arrested.  
1 CONT The above facts were gathered by our reporter on the ground yesterday.  Both men were well known to all of the older residents of the county.  Mestas was a local politician of some prominence.  No ill feeling exists among any othe
1 CONC r parties as a result of this tragedy the quarrel having been purely a personal one.
1 CONT
1 CONT March 12, 1884:
1 CONT John Jackson who killed Felipe Mestas on Saturday afternoon came in today and surrendered to Judge Walker.  Subpoenas have been issued for witnesses and the preliminary investigation has been set for 2 o'clock p.m. tomorrow.  
1 CONT
1 CONT John Jackson was arraigned on the charge of manslaughter and plead not guilty.
1 CONT
1 CONT March 13, 1884:
1 CONT John Jackson is confident that he will be vindicated when he is tried and says he would be glad to have a hearing at this court.
0 @N6000000002279598188@ NOTE Baptized on September 17, 1854 (3 days) at Abiquiu, New Mexico.
1 CONT Father: Salvador Mestas
1 CONT Mother: Maria Viterba (Trujillo?)
1 CONT Paternal Grandparents: Rafael Mestas and Maria de la Luz Mascarenas.
1 CONT Maternal Grandparents: Rafael Trujillo and Trinidad Valdes
1 CONT Padrino: Jose Nestor Quintana.
1 CONT Madrina: Josefa Quintana.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ***Juan Maria Mestas was the original Mestas Family genealogist due to his record keeping entries "Nacidos y Muertos" that he had handwritten in his spiral notebook entitled "Libro de Fechas y Edades del ano de Dios del 1880."  Wi
1 CONC thin this document multiple notations, concerning the births and deaths of Mestas family members and extended family and friends, were entered and kept.  
1 CONT After his death, it has been said that the journal (with new entries each time it was transfered) was handed down to his daughter Altagracia (Mestas) Montoya, then his son Juan Salvador Mestas and finally to Juan Salvador's son Al
1 CONC fredo "Freddie" Mestas.  After Freddie's death the journal was left in the care of his wife Aurora (Montez) Mestas with a final "transfer for study" of the document to Orlando Ricardo Mestas.  Orlando is the son of Daniel Mestas w
1 CONC ho was a first cousin to Freddie Mestas and child of Juan Maria Mestas's son Silverio Mestas.
1 CONT This precious family heirloom has formed the basis and foundation for the current Mestas Family Genealogy started in December 2005 by Juan Maria's Great Grandson Orlando Ricardo Mestas who considers it an honor and privelege to ca
1 CONC rry on the historical record keeping tradition first started by his Great Grandfather Juan Maria Mestas in 1880.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage Padrinos: Juan Pablo Gallegos and Maria Cruz Benavidez.
1 CONT Juan Maria was a pioneer Spanish-American resident of Las Animas county for fifty years.
1 CONT He died at 9:30 in the morning at his home in the "Sunflower Valley".
1 CONT Buried: June 9, 1927 in the Mestas Cemetery east of Trinidad, Colorado near Hoehne.
