0 @N6000000002279598064@ NOTE Baptized May 7, 1898, Trinidad, Colorado.
1 CONT Padrinos: Refugio Sanchiz and Maria Concepcion de Sanchiz (Torres).
1 CONT Silverio didn't like to have pictures taken of him.
1 CONT Silverio and Fructosa Mestas were godparents to "Silver Gurule" who was named after Silverio.
1 CONT Silverio and his wife Fructosa used to listen to "Eddie Arnold", country western music,  from Dalhart, Texas and World War II news, when their son Daniel was in the war overseas, on the family radio.
1 CONT This radio, now an antique, has been passed on to Daniel Mestas's son Orlando.
1 CONT Silverio worked as ranch hand and laborer at the Ernest G. Myers Ranch in Hoehne, Colorado along with his brother Salvador Mestas where he worked from December 1922 until March 1947 just shortly before his death.
1 CONT Exempt from World War I due to "Sole Provider Status".
1 CONT Buried: August 26, 1947, Hoehne Cemetery, Hoehne, Colorado and is buried next to his sister Altagracia Mestas Montoya who died in 1960.
1 CONT Silverio died at the home of his son Daniel Mestas at 517 Convent Street, Trinidad, Colorado when he was 49 years, 4 months and 19 days old.  Death was due to Cerebral Hemorrhage, Arteriosclerosis.
1 CONT Had been ill since February of 1947.
